tion></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Horn, R., S. Ding, and H.J. Gruber. <year>2000</year>. Immobilizing the moving parts of voltage-gated ion channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>116</volume>:<fpage>461</fpage>&#x02013;475.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10962021</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Islas, L.D., and F.J. Sigworth. <year>2001</year>. Elecrostatics and the gating pore of Shaker potassium channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>117</volume>:<fpage>69</fpage>&#x02013;89.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11134232</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Jiang, Y., A. Lee, J. Chen, M. Cadene, B.T. Chait, and R. MacKinnon. <year>2002</year>. The open pore conformation of potassium channels. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>417</volume>:<fpage>523</fpage>&#x02013;526.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12037560</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Larsson, H.P., O.S. Baker, D.S. Dhillon, and E.Y. Isacoff. <year>1996</year>. Transmembrane movement of the <italic>Shaker</italic> K<sup>+</sup> channel S4. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>16</volume>:<fpage>387</fpage>&#x02013;397.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8789953</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Li-Smerin, Y., D.H. Hackos, and K.J. Swartz. <year>2000</year>. Alpha-helical structural elements within the voltage-sensing domains of a K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>115</volume>:<fpage>33</fpage>&#x02013;50.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10613917</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Li-Smerin, Y., and K. Swartz. <year>2001</year>. Helical structure of the COOH terminus of S3 and its contribution to the gating modifier toxin receptor in voltage-gated ion channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>113</volume>:<fpage>415</fpage>&#x02013;423.</citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Mannuzzu, L.M., M.M. Moronne, and E.Y. Isacoff. <year>1996</year>. Direct physical measure of conformational rearrangement underlying potassium channel gating. <source>Science.</source> <volume>271</volume>:<fpage>213</fpage>&#x02013;216.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8539623</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Monks, S.A., D.J. Needleman, and C. Miller. <year>1999</year>. Helical structure and packing orientation of the S2 segment in the <italic>Shaker</italic> K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>113</volume>:<fpage>415</fpage>&#x02013;423.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10051517</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Noceti, F., P. Baldelli, X. Wei, N. Qin, L. Toro, L. Birnbaumer, and E. Stefani. <year>1996</year>. Effective gating charges per channel in voltage-dependent K<sup>+</sup> and Ca<sup>2+</sup> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>108</volume>:<fpage>143</fpage>&#x02013;155.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8882860</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Papazian, D.M., X.M. Shao, S.-A. Seoh, A.F. Mock, Y. Huang, and D.H. Wainstock. <year>1995</year>. Electrostatic interactions of S4 voltage sensor in <italic>Shaker</italic> K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>14</volume>:<fpage>1293</fpage>&#x02013;1301.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7605638</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Perozo, E., D.M. Cortes, and L.G. Cuello. <year>1999</year>. Structural rearrangements underlying K<sup>+</sup> channel activation gating. <source>Science.</source> <volume>285</volume>:<fpage>73</fpage>&#x02013;78.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10390363</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Ren, D., B. Navarro, H. Xu, L. Yue, Q. Shi, and D.E. Clapham. <year>2001</year>. A prokaryotic voltage-gated sodium channel. <source>Science.</source> <volume>294</volume>:<fpage>2372</fpage>&#x02013;2375.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11743207</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Roux, B. <year>2002</year>. What can be deduced about the structure of Shaker from available data? <source>Novartis Found. Symp.</source> <volume>245</volume>:<fpage>84</fpage>&#x02013;101.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12027017</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Schoppa, N.E., K. McCormack, M.A. Tanouye, and F.J. Sigworth. <year>1992</year>. The size of gating charge in wild-type and mutant Shaker potassium channels. <source>Science.</source> <volume>255</volume>:<fpage>1712</fpage>&#x02013;1715.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1553560</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Selvin, P.R. <year>1996</year>. Lanthanide-based resonance energy transfer. <source>IEEE J. of Selected Topics in Quantum Electronics.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>1077</fpage>&#x02013;1087.</citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Seoh, S.-A., D. Sigg, D.M. Papazian, and F. Bezanilla. <year>1996</year>. Voltage-sensing residues in the S2 and S4 segments of the <italic>Shaker</italic> K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>16</volume>:<fpage>1159</fpage>&#x02013;1167.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8663992</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="other">Silverman, W.R. 2002. Structural interactions in the voltage sensor of potassium channels derived from metal ion coordination in Ether-a-go-go. PhD Thesis. University of California at Los Angeles, CA.</citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation citation-type="journal">Starace, D.M., and F. Bezanilla. <year>1999</year>. Histidine at position 362 causes inwardly rectifying H<sup>+</sup> conduction in Shaker K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>76</volume>:<fpage>A266</fpage>.</citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="journal">Starace, D.M., and F. Bezanilla. <year>2001</year>a. Histidine scanning mutagenesis of uncharged residues of the Shaker K+ channel S4 segment. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>80</volume>:<fpage>217a</fpage>.</citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><citation citation-type="journal">Starace, D.M., and F. Bezanilla. <year>2001</year>b. Histidine scanning mutagenesis of basic residues of the S4 segment of the Shaker K+ channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>117</volume>:<fpage>469</fpage>&#x02013;490.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11331357</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><citation citation-type="journal">Starace, D.M., E. Stefani, and F. Bezanilla. <year>1997</year>. Voltage-dependent proton transport by the voltage sensor of the Shaker K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>19</volume>:<fpage>1319</fpage>&#x02013;1327.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9427254</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><citation citation-type="journal">Starace, D.M., P.R. Selvin, and F. Bezanilla. <year>2002</year>. Resonance energy transfer measurements of transmembrane motion of Shaker K+ channel voltage sensing region. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>82</volume>:<fpage>174a</fpage>.</citation></ref><ref id="bib37"><citation citation-type="journal">Tiwari-Woodruff, S.K., M.A. Lin, C.T. Schulteis, and D.M. Papazian. <year>2000</year>. Voltage-dependent structural interactions in the <italic>Shaker</italic> K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>115</volume>:<fpage>123</fpage>&#x02013;138.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10653892</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib38"><citation citation-type="journal">Yang, N., A.L. George, and R. Horn. <year>1996</year>. Molecular basis of charge movement in voltage-gated sodium channels. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>16</volume>:<fpage>113</fpage>&#x02013;122.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8562074</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib39"><citation citation-type="journal">Yusaf, S.P., D. Wray, and A. Sivaprasadarao. <year>1996</year>. Measurement of the movement of the S4 segment during activation of a voltage-gated potassium channel. <source>Pflugers Arc. Eur. J. Physiol.</source> <volume>433</volume>:<fpage>91</fpage>&#x02013;97.</citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="fn1"><label>1</label><p>The simple rotational model including the simulation of single gating shots, single channels, gating currents, and ionic currents is available at: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://pb010.anes.ucla.edu/model/rotmodel.html">http://pb010.anes.ucla.edu/model/rotmodel.html</ext-link>.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>